00:40:35.580 --> 00:40:39.143
are still identified with their childhood vulnerabilities.

00:40:40.130 --> 00:40:42.710
And so I'm never gonna make myself powerless again.

00:40:42.710 --> 00:40:44.110
They think that powerlessness

00:40:44.110 --> 00:40:48.690
in that sense opens you up to being hurt.

00:40:48.690 --> 00:40:49.560
We're actually...

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:52.830
That surrender is actually the way of, out of the hurt.

00:40:52.830 --> 00:40:54.540
But let me come back to you just for a moment.

00:40:54.540 --> 00:40:57.020
So this ethic that you had that I need to do this myself

00:40:57.020 --> 00:40:58.590
and I can do this and so on.

00:40:58.590 --> 00:40:59.740
Well, think about that.

00:41:01.610 --> 00:41:03.850
Do you want your kids to have that?

00:41:03.850 --> 00:41:04.810
No.

00:41:04.810 --> 00:41:08.223
How would you make sure that they don't?

00:41:09.686 --> 00:41:12.410
I would imagine by trying to help them

00:41:12.410 --> 00:41:17.060
to understand that making your way in the world

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:19.450
is an interdependent act.

00:41:19.450 --> 00:41:23.490
That the way the path towards whatever your goal is

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:26.380
or your dreams, your aspirations for your life

00:41:26.380 --> 00:41:29.970
is contingent upon the community that you cultivate.

00:41:29.970 --> 00:41:32.400
Okay, so that's intellectually too,

00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:36.430
but how will you support that understanding in them?

00:41:40.820 --> 00:41:44.980
I think by trying to help them foster an ethic

00:41:44.980 --> 00:41:49.160
of open communication and feeling safe with communicating.

00:41:49.160 --> 00:41:50.130
With whom?

00:41:50.130 --> 00:41:53.208
Well, with myself and my wife first and foremost,

00:41:53.208 --> 00:41:54.210
and then their siblings

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:55.950
and then extrapolating out from there.

00:41:55.950 --> 00:41:57.560
That's the whole point.

00:41:57.560 --> 00:41:58.860
So that's what you lacked.

00:42:00.070 --> 00:42:02.400
And then we developed a coping mechanism,

00:42:02.400 --> 00:42:04.610
I can do this all by myself.

00:42:04.610 --> 00:42:05.510
No.

00:42:05.510 --> 00:42:08.000
It is true that you want to foster self-reliance

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:12.683
and internal resilience and initiative, that's true.

00:42:14.080 --> 00:42:18.120
But the pathway towards doing that is the same

00:42:18.120 --> 00:42:21.450
as the pathway towards fostering relational health

00:42:25.240 --> 00:42:27.620
which is by giving the children the environment

00:42:27.620 --> 00:42:29.290
where they can actually feel listened to

00:42:29.290 --> 00:42:32.603
and validated and supported.

00:42:33.850 --> 00:42:36.133
And this is what so many of us didn't get.

00:42:38.440 --> 00:42:40.890
It might be interesting for you at some point

00:42:40.890 --> 00:42:43.630
to speak with your parents and if depending

00:42:43.630 --> 00:42:45.470
on the relationship that you have with them say,

00:42:45.470 --> 00:42:46.940
okay look, just tell me about your marriage

00:42:46.940 --> 00:42:47.950
when I was a kid.

00:42:47.950 --> 00:42:49.880
Not mine what you didn't do for me,

00:42:49.880 --> 00:42:51.160
like I know you did your very best,

00:42:51.160 --> 00:42:53.210
I love you very much, I honor you.

00:42:53.210 --> 00:42:54.810
But tell me about your lives.

00:42:54.810 --> 00:42:56.610
What was it like for you?

00:42:56.610 --> 00:42:57.920
What stresses were you under?

00:42:57.920 --> 00:42:59.770
What were you carrying from your own childhood?

00:42:59.770 --> 00:43:00.603
Yeah, I mean I can-

00:43:00.603 --> 00:43:02.500
What was happening in your larger family,

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:04.230
were there work stresses?

00:43:04.230 --> 00:43:06.340
Just what, how was your relationship?

00:43:06.340 --> 00:43:07.420
All those questions.

00:43:07.420 --> 00:43:09.770
Yeah I mean, I think in my mother's case

00:43:09.770 --> 00:43:14.190
she suffered a tremendous amount of trauma in her life.

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:17.360
She lost her father when she was still in college

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:19.370
and then when I was very young,

00:43:19.370 --> 00:43:21.360
she lost her brother in a car accident.

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:23.083
Oh my God, now you mentioning-

00:43:23.083 --> 00:43:24.610
It's tremendous amount of pain in her life

00:43:24.610 --> 00:43:28.240
and stress and it's manifested in stress and anxiety

00:43:28.240 --> 00:43:33.240
and being somebody who is very risk averse.

00:43:33.730 --> 00:43:38.110
And I think that that manifested in my life as a parent

00:43:38.110 --> 00:43:41.680
who was perhaps over-protective.

00:43:41.680 --> 00:43:43.160
However, there's no such thing as over protective,

00:43:43.160 --> 00:43:44.753
you can either protective or not protective.

00:43:44.753 --> 00:43:46.803
What you're talking about is controlling.

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:49.963
Now that comes out of anxiety.

00:43:50.970 --> 00:43:54.130
She was anxious so she wanted to protect you by controlling.

00:43:54.130 --> 00:43:57.493
But the impact on you is loss of autonomy.

00:44:01.210 --> 00:44:03.130
And just imagine your poor mother state of mind

00:44:03.130 --> 00:44:04.830
when her brother dies.

00:44:04.830 --> 00:44:08.270
She just lost her father not that many years before,

00:44:08.270 --> 00:44:11.230
now she's a relatively young mom and she loses her brother

00:44:11.230 --> 00:44:12.243
in a car accident.

00:44:13.200 --> 00:44:16.136
And then she's got the small child to look after.

00:44:16.136 --> 00:44:18.390
What could be your state of mind?

00:44:18.390 --> 00:44:20.850
I mean it's informed her world view

00:44:20.850 --> 00:44:25.250
and I think that made her kind of terrified

00:44:25.250 --> 00:44:28.000
of externalities that she can't control.

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:30.333
But it also just a state of mind.

00:44:31.340 --> 00:44:34.030
Would it be sadness, grief,

00:44:34.030 --> 00:44:37.053
maybe depression, a lot of stress.

00:44:38.287 --> 00:44:42.810
And sadness, grief, depression, stress acts

00:44:42.810 --> 00:44:45.700
as a screen between the parent's love

00:44:45.700 --> 00:44:49.160
for the child and the child.

00:44:49.160 --> 00:44:52.860
And it's nothing that the parent is doing on purpose.

00:44:52.860 --> 00:44:56.340
So we know for example that postpartum depression

00:44:56.340 --> 00:44:58.360
is a significant risk factor

00:44:58.360 --> 00:45:00.283
for childhood developmental problems.

00:45:01.970 --> 00:45:02.830
It's got nothing to do with

00:45:02.830 --> 00:45:07.100
how much the mother loves the child, how devoted she is.

00:45:07.100 --> 00:45:10.290
It just children pick up

00:45:10.290 --> 00:45:14.370
on their parents' emotions automatically and they imbibe,

00:45:14.370 --> 00:45:16.100
they absorb the parents' emotions

00:45:16.100 --> 00:45:17.650
and they think it's about them.

00:45:18.600 --> 00:45:21.240
So when my mother's side as my mother was

00:45:21.240 --> 00:45:23.840
because of the situation as I write about in the book

00:45:23.840 --> 00:45:24.760
in the second world war,

00:45:24.760 --> 00:45:29.760
a Jewish family in Nazi occupied Hungary.

00:45:30.100 --> 00:45:34.920
Her grief and her own happiness to the infant means

00:45:34.920 --> 00:45:36.553
that she's not happy with me.

00:45:38.470 --> 00:45:40.720
So my self esteem is undermined.

00:45:40.720 --> 00:45:41.900
Not 'cause my mother didn't love me,

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:43.750
she love me fiercely and desperately.

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:49.940
But just because children absorb

00:45:50.820 --> 00:45:54.030
and are shaped by the emotional states of their parents.

00:45:54.030 --> 00:45:57.710
And those emotional states are not deliberate

00:45:57.710 --> 00:45:59.630
on the part of the parent.

00:45:59.630 --> 00:46:04.630
And your mother was not a grief struck grieving young mother

00:46:05.200 --> 00:46:06.450
because she wanted to be.

00:46:07.810 --> 00:46:09.830
But the fact that she was.

00:46:09.830 --> 00:46:14.830
And furthermore, if you were living in a clan based society

00:46:16.980 --> 00:46:21.980
where there's a lot of aunts, uncles, elders, neighbors,

00:46:22.210 --> 00:46:25.000
even living under the same roof perhaps.

00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:27.280
Well then that mother who has suffered

00:46:27.280 --> 00:46:29.590
that loss has got a lot of support.

00:46:29.590 --> 00:46:31.970
And the impact of that one singular person

00:46:31.970 --> 00:46:35.810
on a young child's life is diminished and replaced

00:46:35.810 --> 00:46:39.080
in some part by other people that can pick up the slack

00:46:39.080 --> 00:46:42.310
and provide a tapestry of emotional support.

00:46:42.310 --> 00:46:43.143
This is how you're right

00:46:43.143 --> 00:46:45.050
and that's why it takes a village.

00:46:45.050 --> 00:46:46.310
Yeah it does.

00:46:46.310 --> 00:46:49.180
We try to cultivate that with our kids too,

00:46:49.180 --> 00:46:52.690
we have a very much of a open house kind of situation

00:46:52.690 --> 00:46:55.070
with lots of people coming in and there's a lot of adults

00:46:55.070 --> 00:46:57.100
that are around our kids for that very reason.

00:46:57.100 --> 00:46:58.480
Wonderful, yeah which is wonderful.

00:46:58.480 --> 00:47:00.080
And we've moved away from that,

00:47:00.080 --> 00:47:00.970
it is true and it's...

00:47:00.970 --> 00:47:02.756
It's a society gap.

00:47:02.756 --> 00:47:03.970
In this internet culture

00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:08.070
of seemingly never being more connected than we are,

00:47:08.070 --> 00:47:11.970
we're lonelier and more isolated than ever.

00:47:11.970 --> 00:47:14.530
Yeah, so then we talk about why are...

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:17.360
Yeah, so to go back to your original point.

00:47:17.360 --> 00:47:19.640
Yes, there are very few children in our society

00:47:19.640 --> 00:47:23.380
who are not significantly affected

00:47:23.380 --> 00:47:26.983
by these dynamics regardless of the parents' best efforts.

00:47:29.340 --> 00:47:32.240
Now, until we get conscious around it and it sounds like you

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:35.030
and your parenting had developed some consciousness.

00:47:35.030 --> 00:47:38.230
But most of us just sort of try and get by.

00:47:38.230 --> 00:47:39.430
You're just trying to pay the bills

00:47:39.430 --> 00:47:41.750
and put food on the plate.

00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:43.320
And to keep the relationship going

00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:46.540
and do the right thing and we're not conscious

00:47:46.540 --> 00:47:49.533
of what we've lost as a society.

00:47:50.834 --> 00:47:53.540
And your own consciousness was probably gained at

00:47:55.950 --> 00:47:58.700
or through dealing with your own addictions.

00:47:58.700 --> 00:48:00.690
You're probably much more conscious as a parent.

00:48:00.690 --> 00:48:01.523
Oh of course.

00:48:01.523 --> 00:48:06.523
And it's that adage of being the grateful alcoholic

00:48:07.040 --> 00:48:12.040
because it's catapulted me into a life based more

00:48:12.320 --> 00:48:14.320
on spiritual principles than maybe

00:48:14.320 --> 00:48:16.770
or perhaps I would have ever been introduced to

00:48:16.770 --> 00:48:21.300
but when I went away to college, I went the other coast

00:48:21.300 --> 00:48:24.910
and that's when I discovered drugs and alcohol.

00:48:24.910 --> 00:48:27.810
And that was the solution that I wasn't the problem,

00:48:27.810 --> 00:48:31.690
that was what made me feel whole until it played itself out

00:48:31.690 --> 00:48:34.070
and took me to some dark places.

00:48:34.070 --> 00:48:36.403
But as that kind of unfolded,

00:48:38.710 --> 00:48:42.250
predictably that's where the shame and the low self-esteem

00:48:42.250 --> 00:48:44.640
and the self-judgment and all of that comes in as you start

00:48:44.640 --> 00:48:48.460
to bifurcate into this kind of double life existence

00:48:48.460 --> 00:48:51.790
that further isolates you and alienates you

00:48:51.790 --> 00:48:53.310
from your community.

00:48:53.310 --> 00:48:55.800
I'm almost impelled to say that you were fortunate

00:48:55.800 --> 00:48:59.330
to have become an alcoholic because you see my addictions

00:48:59.330 --> 00:49:03.300
to work and to shopping, they're respectable ones.

00:49:03.300 --> 00:49:07.130
You don't suffer the consequences to the same degree

00:49:07.130 --> 00:49:09.380
so that you don't get the same wake up call.

00:49:09.380 --> 00:49:12.780
No, I'm not wishing I wasn't, I'd been an alcoholic

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:15.830
but actually if I'm honest about it,

00:49:15.830 --> 00:49:17.980
sometimes when I meet people who've been through recovery

00:49:17.980 --> 00:49:21.510
from severe addiction, there's a sense of envy almost

00:49:21.510 --> 00:49:23.430
because they've really been forced

00:49:23.430 --> 00:49:28.400
by their intense suffering to go very deep with themselves

00:49:28.400 --> 00:49:30.950
Well pain is the only thing

00:49:30.950 --> 00:49:33.510
that really motivates people to change their ways right.

00:49:33.510 --> 00:49:35.580
So how much pain can you bear before?

00:49:35.580 --> 00:49:38.720
I know but unfortunately it was only pain,

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:39.820
there'd be a lot less suffering.

00:49:39.820 --> 00:49:42.593
It takes more than just pain but yeah, it's true.

00:49:44.260 --> 00:49:48.090
There's a line I'm reading in a Greek play I was looking

00:49:48.090 --> 00:49:51.683
through recently where they say we suffer into truth.

00:49:53.180 --> 00:49:55.220
We suffer into truth and so the more intense

00:49:55.220 --> 00:49:56.520
the suffering sometimes the deeper

00:49:56.520 --> 00:49:57.690
the appreciation of truth.

00:49:57.690 --> 00:50:00.240
And a lot of the spiritual teachers will tell you that.

00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:01.920
I'm reading Eckhart Tolle these days

00:50:01.920 --> 00:50:06.450
and with a lot of gratitude.

00:50:06.450 --> 00:50:11.450
And he talks about how sometimes the suffering is so intense

00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:13.770
that it just burns away the ego.

00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:16.853
And then you get the truth.

00:50:17.870 --> 00:50:19.130
Yeah but I would say also,

00:50:19.130 --> 00:50:21.250
I'm interested in your perspective on this.

00:50:21.250 --> 00:50:26.250
So, I get sober and my sobriety is buttressed by community

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:30.170
and the 12 steps and the principles and the sort of work

00:50:30.170 --> 00:50:33.530
that I do in that program service oriented

00:50:33.530 --> 00:50:35.580
and sort of living my life premised

00:50:35.580 --> 00:50:38.480
on these spiritual principles that have really given me

00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:40.460
this amazing life that I'm very grateful for

00:50:40.460 --> 00:50:45.460
and yet at the same time, I'm certainly not cured.

00:50:46.830 --> 00:50:50.370
It's like having all of these things in place

00:50:50.370 --> 00:50:54.860
to keep me walking the correct path.

00:50:54.860 --> 00:50:57.803
It's still a process.

00:50:57.803 --> 00:50:59.473
What would cure mean to you?

00:51:00.700 --> 00:51:05.700
Cured would mean that that's the impulse to drink

00:51:06.550 --> 00:51:11.330
or use or to try to check out of the present

00:51:11.330 --> 00:51:14.440
through any number of behavior patterns

00:51:14.440 --> 00:51:18.720
that are inconsistent with how I would like to live my life

00:51:18.720 --> 00:51:20.400
that create negative consequences

00:51:20.400 --> 00:51:23.100
no longer enter my consciousness.

00:51:23.100 --> 00:51:24.680
So you would like a Jesus or whatever-

00:51:24.680 --> 00:51:25.513
(Rich laughs)

00:51:25.513 --> 00:51:26.660
Yeah.
If there's a high ambition.

00:51:26.660 --> 00:51:27.493
I would.

00:51:27.493 --> 00:51:28.326
Yeah, okay.

00:51:29.160 --> 00:51:31.120
Well, good luck with that.

00:51:31.120 --> 00:51:35.250
But I mean kind of in brass tacks day to day life,

00:51:35.250 --> 00:51:40.250
you know gratitude is a fleeting experience for me.

00:51:40.440 --> 00:51:42.110
My default state is not gratitude,

00:51:42.110 --> 00:51:44.900
my default state is irritability, anxiety,

00:51:44.900 --> 00:51:46.630
judgement, frustration.

00:51:46.630 --> 00:51:50.070
Yeah, my default state is irritability as well.

00:51:50.070 --> 00:51:53.600
Well now look, what you're talking about now is...

00:51:56.037 --> 00:52:00.010
First of all let's just eliminate the word cure here.

00:52:01.120 --> 00:52:03.770
And nothing will cure, we're talking about wholeness.

00:52:04.690 --> 00:52:07.830
And it just means you've got more work to do.

00:52:07.830 --> 00:52:11.140
So it's a question of how committed you are to the work.

00:52:11.140 --> 00:52:14.870
And I know for me, those are my default states as well,

00:52:14.870 --> 00:52:17.090
I can't help that, I can't help it that's my default state,

00:52:17.090 --> 00:52:21.323
I didn't create that, that's a consequence of experiences.

00:52:22.450 --> 00:52:24.860
What I can make a choice about is how I relate

00:52:24.860 --> 00:52:27.560
to all that and how I live my life

00:52:27.560 --> 00:52:32.560
and what structures supports, practices do I engage in

00:52:37.180 --> 00:52:41.560
that helps me deal with that irritability when it arises.

00:52:41.560 --> 00:52:45.180
That urge to check out when it arises.

00:52:45.180 --> 00:52:46.763
So that's very much up to me.

00:52:51.371 --> 00:52:55.610
This is my 4th book and it's had tremendous response

00:52:55.610 --> 00:53:00.610
and as have my others and I travel a lot speaking all

00:53:01.210 --> 00:53:04.230
over North America and internationally.

00:53:04.230 --> 00:53:06.260
And very often people have said to me, boy,

00:53:06.260 --> 00:53:07.870
I wish I had read your book 30 years ago

00:53:07.870 --> 00:53:11.700
and my joke always was, gee, maybe I should read it myself

00:53:11.700 --> 00:53:14.620
because on the one hand I can say these things

00:53:14.620 --> 00:53:16.370
and teach people and help people

00:53:16.370 --> 00:53:18.010
but there was this tremendous gap

00:53:18.010 --> 00:53:20.390
between my internal and personal life.

00:53:20.390 --> 00:53:24.283
And what I can manifest on the stage or in a seminar room.

00:53:25.691 --> 00:53:28.950
And I can say no, but I'm 71.

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:33.950
And I can say now that that gap is narrowing significantly.

00:53:35.510 --> 00:53:38.230
But that's because I'm actually doing the work.

00:53:38.230 --> 00:53:40.817
Yeah, the aspiration is the commitment to the work

00:53:40.817 --> 00:53:42.400
and to the process, right?

00:53:42.400 --> 00:53:44.870
So if you're doing the work,

00:53:44.870 --> 00:53:49.870
then inevitably it'll pay off and it's not helpful

00:53:50.760 --> 00:53:52.510
to talk to yourself about...

00:53:52.510 --> 00:53:54.720
To talk about yourself as not being cured,

00:53:54.720 --> 00:53:57.670
you're not a piece of meat, you don't have to be cured.

00:53:57.670 --> 00:54:00.423
You're just need to integrate.

00:54:01.790 --> 00:54:04.800
And that's a lifelong process and again,

00:54:04.800 --> 00:54:08.010
as Eckhart Tolle says that for some people it happens

00:54:08.010 --> 00:54:11.470
as a sudden illumination.

00:54:11.470 --> 00:54:13.570
That's essentially what happened to him.

00:54:13.570 --> 00:54:17.050
But for more people, it's work, it's ongoing work.

00:54:17.050 --> 00:54:20.090
Yeah I mean self knowledge is insufficient,

00:54:20.090 --> 00:54:21.910
I could read your book cover to cover,

00:54:21.910 --> 00:54:24.020
I could read it every week for the rest of my life

00:54:24.020 --> 00:54:27.050
but if I don't extract from your book principles

00:54:27.050 --> 00:54:29.840
that can translate into actions that I can take

00:54:29.840 --> 00:54:32.793
on a daily basis to narrow that gap,

00:54:32.793 --> 00:54:37.380
then it's not really going to help me or anyone else.

00:54:37.380 --> 00:54:39.480
It's the practice.
Yeah.

00:54:39.480 --> 00:54:41.830
And I was pointed in another dimension there.

00:54:41.830 --> 00:54:44.020
Is that there's a difference of in self knowledge

00:54:44.020 --> 00:54:45.973
and knowledge about the self.

00:54:46.979 --> 00:54:48.260
Explain that.

00:54:48.260 --> 00:54:51.120
Well, you can know about yourself intellectually

00:54:51.120 --> 00:54:52.860
but that wouldn't be self knowledge,

00:54:52.860 --> 00:54:54.423
self knowledge is experiential.

00:54:55.766 --> 00:54:58.760
We actually experienced you to self, that self knowledge.

00:54:58.760 --> 00:55:01.280
It's got nothing to do with the intellect.

00:55:01.280 --> 00:55:04.733
And you're totally right,

00:55:07.040 --> 00:55:08.290
I could read all these wonderful books

00:55:08.290 --> 00:55:10.580
that I have over the years, make dozens.

00:55:10.580 --> 00:55:14.360
I could write these books full of insight

00:55:14.360 --> 00:55:17.963
and I'd say wisdom and finance.

00:55:19.560 --> 00:55:21.100
That's not self knowledge.

00:55:21.100 --> 00:55:22.893
That's knowledge about the self.

00:55:24.560 --> 00:55:29.560
And it's a different level of knowing because we have...

00:55:31.420 --> 00:55:32.253
There's different levels of knowing,

00:55:32.253 --> 00:55:34.330
there's the intellectual knowing,

00:55:34.330 --> 00:55:37.330
there's the intuitive knowing

00:55:37.330 --> 00:55:39.123
and there's experiential knowing.

00:55:40.060 --> 00:55:45.000
And the deepest one is the experiential one

00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:49.113
that you've actually, yes, this is the self, here I am.

00:55:50.650 --> 00:55:53.930
And even then that takes practice to maintain

00:55:53.930 --> 00:55:57.223
that space because the world's so quickly robs you of it.

00:56:03.070 --> 00:56:07.900
I think alcoholism and addiction are ciphers

00:56:07.900 --> 00:56:12.530
to compel one to face that and walk that path

00:56:12.530 --> 00:56:14.070
and undertake that journey.

00:56:14.070 --> 00:56:14.903
Yes.

00:56:15.770 --> 00:56:18.890
But for, I'm putting myself in the shoes

00:56:18.890 --> 00:56:20.260
of somebody who might be listening

00:56:20.260 --> 00:56:23.510
who is not afflicted in that way or at least on a,

00:56:23.510 --> 00:56:25.914
I mean in your mind, it's a spectrum and-

00:56:25.914 --> 00:56:27.810
They fit in in a different way.

00:56:27.810 --> 00:56:30.610
Different way perhaps less severe

00:56:30.610 --> 00:56:34.570
the idea of experiential self knowledge

00:56:34.570 --> 00:56:37.630
or what that journey would look like is a foreign concept

00:56:37.630 --> 00:56:39.840
to a lot of people like how does one even begin

00:56:39.840 --> 00:56:44.050
to crack the seal on what that means on a day-to-day basis?

00:56:44.050 --> 00:56:46.333
Well, look, I mean, the Buddha,

00:56:49.670 --> 00:56:51.500
when he sat under the Bodhi tree

00:56:51.500 --> 00:56:54.350
and meditated all those days after having followed this

00:56:54.350 --> 00:56:57.710
or that practice of severe abstention

00:56:57.710 --> 00:57:02.710
or self flagellation and spiritual exploration.

00:57:08.170 --> 00:57:12.780
He was dealing with the problem of human suffering,

00:57:12.780 --> 00:57:15.800
which is not restricted to specific addicts only,

00:57:15.800 --> 00:57:18.070
it's the human condition because that disconnection

00:57:18.070 --> 00:57:20.890
from the self happens to everybody.

00:57:20.890 --> 00:57:24.230
And everybody suffers.

00:57:24.230 --> 00:57:26.420
Now, we can cover up that suffering

00:57:26.420 --> 00:57:31.160
with the munificence of our consumer society,

00:57:31.160 --> 00:57:33.483
and that's why the desperation by the way.

00:57:35.370 --> 00:57:37.527
The great Henry David Thoreau quote,

00:57:37.527 --> 00:57:40.020
"the massive men lead lives of quiet desperation

00:57:40.020 --> 00:57:41.960
and what is considered resignation

00:57:41.960 --> 00:57:44.470
is confirmed desperation."

00:57:44.470 --> 00:57:45.360
Yeah.

00:57:45.360 --> 00:57:48.240
So I'm not sure that you're right about the listener

00:57:48.240 --> 00:57:50.560
who hasn't suffered addiction and afflicted

00:57:50.560 --> 00:57:53.880
in the same degree, people in all walks

00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:56.520
of lives know somehow...

00:57:56.520 --> 00:57:59.170
And they believe and say, I just don't know myself,

00:57:59.170 --> 00:58:01.240
I don't know who I am, I don't know what's my purpose

00:58:01.240 --> 00:58:04.020
in life and all that so those are all states

00:58:04.020 --> 00:58:05.920
of disconnection from the self,

00:58:05.920 --> 00:58:08.030
very common in our society.

00:58:08.030 --> 00:58:10.630
So I actually think a lot of people would understand

00:58:10.630 --> 00:58:11.880
what we're talking about

00:58:11.880 --> 00:58:15.190
even if they haven't suffered degrees of trauma

00:58:15.190 --> 00:58:18.620
and compensations for trauma such as maybe you have

00:58:18.620 --> 00:58:20.113
or to the degree that I have.

00:58:22.560 --> 00:58:25.210
So in a real-world context,

00:58:25.210 --> 00:58:30.210
the process of beginning to probe into the deeper,

00:58:30.380 --> 00:58:35.380
more authentic self within, what is the practice of that?

00:58:41.270 --> 00:58:42.810
By recognizing...

00:58:42.810 --> 00:58:45.250
Well, again, since I'm reading that Eckhart these days

00:58:45.250 --> 00:58:48.280
I'll quote him, he says that mankind's greatest achievement

00:58:48.280 --> 00:58:52.980
is not our architecture and our science and our creations

00:58:52.980 --> 00:58:55.720
but the recognition of our own insanity.

00:58:55.720 --> 00:58:58.500
So, the first thing is you gotta get

00:58:58.500 --> 00:58:59.660
that it's not working for you.

00:58:59.660 --> 00:59:00.493
Yeah.

00:59:00.493 --> 00:59:02.010
I mean if you don't get that,

00:59:02.010 --> 00:59:04.720
you just continue to tread the same.

00:59:04.720 --> 00:59:06.290
Well, there's so many layers

00:59:06.290 --> 00:59:07.510
that have to be peeled back.

00:59:07.510 --> 00:59:09.197
I mean, we're immersed in...

00:59:09.197 --> 00:59:11.880
And this gets into toxic culture and materialism

00:59:11.880 --> 00:59:14.840
like the society in which we live in is predicated

00:59:14.840 --> 00:59:19.840
upon these ideals that are in certain respects antithetical

00:59:19.955 --> 00:59:24.300
to this journey, we're sort of fed this message

00:59:24.300 --> 00:59:25.860
of climbing the corporate ladder

00:59:25.860 --> 00:59:28.970
and Keeping Up with the Joneses

00:59:28.970 --> 00:59:33.970
in a competitive kind of accumulation focused

00:59:34.710 --> 00:59:37.510
and prioritized way of living

00:59:37.510 --> 00:59:41.220
that is simply fomenting more suffering

00:59:41.220 --> 00:59:44.823
and further divorcing ourselves from that true nature.

00:59:45.900 --> 00:59:47.790
So you have to be able to pierce that

00:59:47.790 --> 00:59:49.373
to even begin I would think.

00:59:52.760 --> 00:59:54.970
You just have to get that you're suffering.

00:59:54.970 --> 00:59:57.560
I mean, you just have to get that, that's the first thing.

00:59:57.560 --> 00:59:58.970
If you don't know that you're suffering,

00:59:58.970 --> 01:00:01.440
you're not gonna pierce anything.

01:00:01.440 --> 01:00:02.980
You're not gonna look for anything.

01:00:02.980 --> 01:00:04.720
So, then you can study.

01:00:04.720 --> 01:00:06.720
And if you realize that you're suffering,

01:00:06.720 --> 01:00:08.960
then you can start asking the question why.

01:00:08.960 --> 01:00:11.038
And then you can look at your own life

01:00:11.038 --> 01:00:14.840
and you look at society and everything else

01:00:14.840 --> 01:00:19.100
but it's gotta begin with some sense

01:00:19.100 --> 01:00:21.450
that things ain't right with you.

01:00:21.450 --> 01:00:22.420
Now, here's...

01:00:23.370 --> 01:00:26.340
I can give you any number of courts.

01:00:26.340 --> 01:00:28.440
The Tibetan Book of Living and Dying talks about

01:00:28.440 --> 01:00:32.110
how the selling of samsara which is of illusion

01:00:32.110 --> 01:00:34.603
is the major function of our society.

01:00:35.440 --> 01:00:39.460
And here's Thomas Merton who is a Catholic monk

01:00:39.460 --> 01:00:44.293
and spiritual teacher who wrote in 1948 or 50s,

01:00:44.293 --> 01:00:45.160
I don't remember this.

01:00:45.160 --> 01:00:48.820
It says it is true that the materialistic society,

01:00:48.820 --> 01:00:50.610
the so-called culture that has evolved

01:00:50.610 --> 01:00:53.640
under the tender mercies of capitalism has produced

01:00:53.640 --> 01:00:57.960
what seems to be the ultimate limit of worldliness

01:00:57.960 --> 01:01:00.880
and nowhere perhaps except in the analogous society

01:01:00.880 --> 01:01:05.210
of pagan room has there ever been such a flooring of cheap

01:01:05.210 --> 01:01:08.890
and petty and disgusting lust and vanities

01:01:08.890 --> 01:01:11.790
in the world of capitalism where there's no evil

01:01:11.790 --> 01:01:13.680
that is not fostered and encouraged

01:01:13.680 --> 01:01:15.263
for the sake of making money.

01:01:16.480 --> 01:01:21.480
And he talks about that we restrain every human limit,

01:01:22.410 --> 01:01:25.573
every desire, every human desire to the limit to create

01:01:27.450 --> 01:01:30.890
as many new desires and synthetic passions as possible

01:01:30.890 --> 01:01:34.780
in order to cater to them with the products of our factories

01:01:34.780 --> 01:01:37.740
and printing presses and movie studios and all the rest.

01:01:37.740 --> 01:01:40.419
This is 60 years before the internet

01:01:40.419 --> 01:01:42.640
or 50 years before the internet.

01:01:42.640 --> 01:01:44.859
So that we live in a society

01:01:44.859 --> 01:01:49.273
that feeds off the hungry ghost.

01:01:50.250 --> 01:01:51.780
And so we keep creating...

01:01:51.780 --> 01:01:53.430
And nobody's does this deliberately,

01:01:53.430 --> 01:01:55.330
although actually they do.

01:01:55.330 --> 01:01:56.480
They did yeah,

01:01:56.480 --> 01:01:58.140
I was gonna take issue with that here.

01:01:58.140 --> 01:01:59.307
But you know what?

01:01:59.307 --> 01:02:01.720
But they can't create the hungry ghost.

01:02:01.720 --> 01:02:03.750
They can cater to it but they can't create it.

01:02:03.750 --> 01:02:06.620
Another it's a Coca Cola company might tell you

01:02:06.620 --> 01:02:09.870
that the greatest joy in the world is to drink a can

01:02:09.870 --> 01:02:11.500
of coke is what they're telling you

01:02:11.500 --> 01:02:14.810
that nothing means as much as drinking a can of coke,

01:02:14.810 --> 01:02:17.560
but you have to be hungry ghost to believe it.

01:02:17.560 --> 01:02:21.140
And then comes in this the persistence

01:02:21.140 --> 01:02:23.850
of this illusion that it will lead to your happiness

01:02:23.850 --> 01:02:24.993
is astonishing.

01:02:24.993 --> 01:02:26.970
Despite never reaching it,

01:02:26.970 --> 01:02:28.600
you think that this is the next time

01:02:28.600 --> 01:02:29.810
where it's actually going to work.

01:02:29.810 --> 01:02:32.350
Well a doctor friend of mine said

01:02:32.350 --> 01:02:34.400
that it's hard to get enough...

01:02:34.400 --> 01:02:38.670
It's difficult to get enough of something that almost works.

01:02:38.670 --> 01:02:40.780
(Rich laughs)

01:02:40.780 --> 01:02:41.750
That's the thing, right?

01:02:41.750 --> 01:02:44.840
If you can come up with that very product,

01:02:44.840 --> 01:02:46.610
then you will be a master of capitalism.

01:02:46.610 --> 01:02:49.690
Yeah, and I can thank you too,

01:02:49.690 --> 01:02:52.100
if only I achieved this, I attain that.

01:02:52.100 --> 01:02:56.150
If my books sell this many copies or if I'm on this program

01:02:57.090 --> 01:02:59.950
or if I buy this many compact disks,

01:02:59.950 --> 01:03:02.500
then I'll be complete and happy.

01:03:02.500 --> 01:03:04.380
it's always that next thing.

01:03:04.380 --> 01:03:05.280
Of course.

01:03:05.280 --> 01:03:06.190
And by the way and there's

01:03:06.190 --> 01:03:10.690
a Buddhist teacher said, just one more is the binding factor

01:03:10.690 --> 01:03:12.315
in the cycle of suffering.

01:03:12.315 --> 01:03:13.810
Right.

01:03:13.810 --> 01:03:17.810
And it leaves me wondering if Eckhart Tolle checks

01:03:17.810 --> 01:03:21.930
his Amazon ranking or has he transcended

01:03:21.930 --> 01:03:24.650
the mortal coil in that regard.

01:03:24.650 --> 01:03:25.927
It would be interesting to find that-

01:03:25.927 --> 01:03:29.030
I mean we all live in the world as does he.

01:03:29.030 --> 01:03:30.660
It'd be interesting to find out,

01:03:30.660 --> 01:03:32.540
my guess is that he would not be checking that.

01:03:32.540 --> 01:03:33.932
Yeah, I would imagine he does that.

01:03:33.932 --> 01:03:35.170
But yeah, just the way I read him

01:03:35.170 --> 01:03:37.741
and not just in his reading but when I see him

01:03:37.741 --> 01:03:40.403
in his videos and one time in person,

01:03:41.460 --> 01:03:43.340
he doesn't strike me that he used to do that.

01:03:43.340 --> 01:03:46.900
And even for myself, I don't need to do it either.

01:03:46.900 --> 01:03:50.910
Not that I don't ever but it fluctuates.

01:03:50.910 --> 01:03:54.630
So the degree to which I'm impelled to do a depends on-

01:03:54.630 --> 01:03:56.340
Well it's a barometer of your spiritual

01:03:56.340 --> 01:03:57.180
and emotional health.

01:03:57.180 --> 01:03:59.830
At that moment, exactly yeah.

01:03:59.830 --> 01:04:02.130
So now I don't think so, I don't think that he would,

01:04:02.130 --> 01:04:03.980
I'd be very much surprised if he did.

01:04:05.570 --> 01:04:07.130
Well we're running out of time and there's

01:04:07.130 --> 01:04:08.660
a couple of things I still wanna talk to you about.

01:04:08.660 --> 01:04:11.577
I mean really kind of the core undercurrence

01:04:11.577 --> 01:04:15.620
of your message with respect to how we kind of treat

01:04:15.620 --> 01:04:18.440
and perceive addicts and addiction culture

01:04:18.440 --> 01:04:22.720
is really a cry for more empathy and love.

01:04:22.720 --> 01:04:26.670
And the idea that a man's natural state

01:04:26.670 --> 01:04:28.630
is one of empathy and love.

01:04:28.630 --> 01:04:32.380
And so when we were talking about how you and I share

01:04:32.380 --> 01:04:35.660
this predisposition to irritability or anxiety

01:04:35.660 --> 01:04:36.583
or what have you-

01:04:37.470 --> 01:04:38.303
Programming.

01:04:38.303 --> 01:04:39.755
Yeah, it's this program.

01:04:39.755 --> 01:04:41.334
I think I heard you in another interview saying

01:04:41.334 --> 01:04:43.293
the idea of second nature came up.

01:04:43.293 --> 01:04:46.700
It's my second nature to be anxious, to be irritable.

01:04:46.700 --> 01:04:50.480
And just let's dissect that phrase second nature

01:04:50.480 --> 01:04:52.590
because it implies that we have a first nature.

01:04:52.590 --> 01:04:55.220
Exactly and then our first nature is according

01:04:55.220 --> 01:04:56.800
to all the scientific research

01:04:58.450 --> 01:05:00.420
or at least overwhelming scientific research

01:05:00.420 --> 01:05:03.770
is that our two natures to be compassionate, to be sharing,

01:05:03.770 --> 01:05:07.540
to be cooperative, to be loving actually.

01:05:07.540 --> 01:05:09.797
None of which is championed in our culture.

01:05:09.797 --> 01:05:11.847
But the opposite is championed.

01:05:14.650 --> 01:05:17.020
So that is our first nature and we suffer precisely

01:05:17.020 --> 01:05:19.780
because we divorced from our first nature.

01:05:19.780 --> 01:05:24.470
Yeah it's that separation from that true state

01:05:24.470 --> 01:05:29.400
that is the kernel of suffering that gets fertilized.

01:05:29.400 --> 01:05:30.233
Yes.

01:05:30.233 --> 01:05:35.000
And so it also means that since it is our first nature,

01:05:35.000 --> 01:05:39.140
then our true nature, even the word recovery,

01:05:39.140 --> 01:05:40.235
what does it mean?

01:05:40.235 --> 01:05:41.260
It mean it was to find something right.

01:05:41.260 --> 01:05:43.220
To return to a natural state.

01:05:43.220 --> 01:05:44.370
Yeah, exactly.

01:05:44.370 --> 01:05:46.420
So the recovery actually is returning to

01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:47.420
who we actually are.

01:05:48.460 --> 01:05:53.460
But the who and the what that we are

01:05:53.650 --> 01:05:55.523
that we got disconnected from.

01:05:56.767 --> 01:05:58.370
And that's what it means to recover.

01:05:58.370 --> 01:06:03.030
And that can be a lifelong process but it's not a bad thing.

01:06:03.030 --> 01:06:04.270
That's not a bad thing.

01:06:04.270 --> 01:06:06.650
It's a worthy journey to undertake.

01:06:06.650 --> 01:06:08.110
'Cause it's not about,

01:06:08.110 --> 01:06:10.250
as with all journeys it's not about...

01:06:12.470 --> 01:06:14.560
If you do it right, it's not about the destination.

01:06:14.560 --> 01:06:17.080
It's about the destination when you get there,

01:06:17.080 --> 01:06:18.421
but it's not about a destination,

01:06:18.421 --> 01:06:20.973
it's about each step along the way.

01:06:22.200 --> 01:06:24.766
And the willingness to undertake that step.

01:06:24.766 --> 01:06:25.599
Yeah.

01:06:27.180 --> 01:06:29.670
And to keep generating that willingness

01:06:29.670 --> 01:06:33.180
'cause I can say I have the willingness Tuesday,

01:06:33.180 --> 01:06:37.155
but it totally absent for me on Wednesday.

01:06:37.155 --> 01:06:40.050
And then I have to look at what's going on here?

01:06:40.050 --> 01:06:44.130
So, it's just a constant willingness to engage

01:06:44.130 --> 01:06:46.440
with however it is at that moment.

01:06:46.440 --> 01:06:48.790
And whenever I've thought of arrived somewhere,

01:06:49.670 --> 01:06:51.360
life will come along and say,

01:06:51.360 --> 01:06:52.830
buddy, you think you have arrived somewhere,

01:06:52.830 --> 01:06:54.210
let me just show you exactly where you're at.

01:06:54.210 --> 01:06:57.730
And then something will happen that will show me exactly.

01:06:57.730 --> 01:06:59.230
Yeah, that mirror will show up.

01:06:59.230 --> 01:07:00.360
The mirror will always shows up

01:07:00.360 --> 01:07:01.630
and it shows up pretty quickly.

01:07:01.630 --> 01:07:02.463
Yes.

01:07:03.680 --> 01:07:06.340
Well, I wanna close it down with one final question,

01:07:06.340 --> 01:07:11.340
which is if you woke up in some bizarre parallel universe

01:07:11.920 --> 01:07:14.840
and found yourself to be a surgeon general,

01:07:14.840 --> 01:07:16.313
in the United States.

01:07:17.880 --> 01:07:20.360
Understanding that the system is what it is

01:07:20.360 --> 01:07:23.370
and is inherently limited in that regard,

01:07:23.370 --> 01:07:28.220
where would your focus be placed in terms of changing policy

01:07:28.220 --> 01:07:31.410
and reframing legislation around recovery and-

01:07:31.410 --> 01:07:32.617
I would pass a law.

01:07:32.617 --> 01:07:33.450
(Rich laughs)

01:07:33.450 --> 01:07:35.290
I would pass a law that says it's illegal

01:07:35.290 --> 01:07:36.533
to use the word addict.

01:07:37.520 --> 01:07:39.210
That every time you wanted to use the word addict,

01:07:39.210 --> 01:07:43.500
instead you have to say he or she,

01:07:43.500 --> 01:07:45.150
you can't use as an addict.

01:07:45.150 --> 01:07:47.950
You'd have to say he or she is a human being

01:07:47.950 --> 01:07:52.380
who suffered immensely and is turned to substances

01:07:52.380 --> 01:07:54.530
or their behaviors to suit their suffering.

01:07:57.270 --> 01:08:01.060
So that every time you thought of addiction or an addict,

01:08:01.060 --> 01:08:02.860
you have to frame it in those terms.

01:08:03.901 --> 01:08:08.240
That would immediately transform the conversation

01:08:08.240 --> 01:08:12.360
and it would immediately be self-evident of how destructive,

01:08:12.360 --> 01:08:17.360
inhumane, retrograde our current attitudes are

01:08:18.077 --> 01:08:19.573
and our current policies are.

01:08:21.070 --> 01:08:23.050
And when I say attitudes and policies,

01:08:23.050 --> 01:08:25.230
I mean the legal ones,

01:08:25.230 --> 01:08:28.313
the psychological ones and the medical ones.

01:08:29.840 --> 01:08:33.380
So, it would be just to reform the conversation,

01:08:33.380 --> 01:08:34.683
reframe the conversation.

01:08:35.910 --> 01:08:37.950
You can say I don't really believe in...

01:08:40.040 --> 01:08:41.623
You can't legislate compassion.

01:08:43.590 --> 01:08:45.320
You can legislate cruelty

01:08:45.320 --> 01:08:47.030
but you can't legislate compassion.

01:08:47.030 --> 01:08:51.470
So, in fact I'm part

01:08:51.470 --> 01:08:54.460
of a new nonprofit called Compassion for Addiction.

01:08:54.460 --> 01:08:56.860
And I think that actually,

01:08:56.860 --> 01:08:59.240
if we adopted that attitude of compassion

01:08:59.240 --> 01:09:02.980
and if we talked about it in the terms that I just outlined,

01:09:02.980 --> 01:09:07.410
we would have not only a much more fruitful approach

01:09:07.410 --> 01:09:08.640
to treating addiction,

01:09:08.640 --> 01:09:10.530
we'd also be a lot happier as a society

01:09:10.530 --> 01:09:13.890
because it will be much more closer to who we really are

01:09:13.890 --> 01:09:16.383
which is compassionate and social minded.

01:09:17.320 --> 01:09:18.680
I love that, that's beautiful.

01:09:18.680 --> 01:09:19.513
Yeah.

01:09:19.513 --> 01:09:20.670
Thanks for talking to me today.

01:09:20.670 --> 01:09:21.790
It's my pleasure, thank you.

01:09:21.790 --> 01:09:23.890
So many things that I had jotted down

01:09:23.890 --> 01:09:25.660
in my notebook here I wanted to talk to you about

01:09:25.660 --> 01:09:27.770
that we didn't even come close to getting to

01:09:27.770 --> 01:09:31.600
so perhaps I can convince you to sit down with me

01:09:31.600 --> 01:09:34.580
at some future point and continue the conversation.

01:09:34.580 --> 01:09:35.413
Yeah, why don't we do that

01:09:35.413 --> 01:09:36.540
when my next book comes up.

01:09:36.540 --> 01:09:38.980
That would be great, happy to do that.

01:09:38.980 --> 01:09:41.220
I appreciate all the work that you're doing,

01:09:41.220 --> 01:09:45.040
it's making a large impact and it is changing lives

01:09:45.040 --> 01:09:49.370
and it's inspiring to hear your message and just thank you.

01:09:49.370 --> 01:09:50.203
Well, thanks so much.

01:09:50.203 --> 01:09:51.947
All right, peace.

01:09:51.947 --> 01:09:53.544
Cuts.

01:09:53.544 --> 01:09:56.211
(upbeat music)

